            ])),
        new("r-b1-5", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Caravanserais: Hotels of the Silk Road",
            "For hundreds of years, merchants travelled along the Silk Road between China and the Mediterranean, carrying silk, spices, paper and glass. The journey could take months, and the roads crossed deserts and mountains. Travellers needed safe places to rest, and so caravanserais appeared.\n\nA caravanserai was a kind of roadside inn. Most were built in a square shape with thick walls and one large gate, which was closed at night to keep out thieves. Inside there was an open courtyard where camels and horses could rest and drink. Around the courtyard were small rooms for travellers, as well as places to store goods.\n\nCaravanserais were usually built about a day's journey apart, roughly thirty or forty kilometres. This meant a caravan could leave one in the morning and reach the next before dark.\n\nBut these buildings were more than hotels. Merchants from different countries met there, exchanged news and did business. Languages, recipes, music and ideas travelled together with the goods.\n\nSome caravanserais still stand in Central Asia, Iran and Turkey today. A few have been restored and now serve as museums, restaurants or even hotels again.",
            [
