                new("Why was the gate closed at night?", ["To keep out thieves", "To keep the camels warm", "To stop merchants from leaving", "To save water"], 0, "Paragraph 2 says the gate \"was closed at night to keep out thieves\"."),
                new("In the last paragraph, what does \"restored\" mean?", ["Destroyed", "Sold to a company", "Moved to another city", "Repaired so that it looks as it did before"], 3, "The old buildings were repaired and are now used again as museums, restaurants or hotels."),
                new("What can we infer from the fourth paragraph?", ["Merchants never spoke to each other", "Caravanserais helped different cultures influence each other", "Only one language was used on the Silk Road", "Music was not allowed in caravanserais"], 1, "It says \"languages, recipes, music and ideas travelled together with the goods\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-1", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Quiet Comeback of Board Games",
            "A decade ago, many people predicted that video games and smartphones would make board games disappear. Instead, the opposite has happened. In cities around the world, board game cafés are opening, crowdfunding websites are full of new game designs, and sales have grown steadily for years. Some shops that once sold only a few classic titles now stock hundreds of games from small independent designers.\n\nPart of the explanation lies in the games themselves. Traditional family games often depended heavily on luck: you rolled the dice, moved your piece and hoped for the best. Many modern games, by contrast, reward planning, negotiation and cooperation. In some of them, players do not compete at all but work together against the game, trying to stop a disease from spreading or to escape from a sinking island before time runs out.\n\nHowever, the social side may be even more important. After a day spent staring at screens, many people are hungry for face-to-face contact. A board game offers a reason to gather around a table, put phones aside and share an experience that does not depend on a strong internet connection. Unlike watching a film together, it requires everyone to take part.\n\nTeachers have noticed the trend too. Some use games to practise maths, languages and problem-solving, arguing that students who would never volunteer an answer in class will happily explain their strategy during a game. A few teachers even design simple games of their own for their classes.\n\nOf course, board games are not replacing digital entertainment; many fans enjoy both. But their revival shows that, even in a highly connected age, people still value the simple pleasure of sitting together and playing.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Board games have become popular again, partly because people want real social contact", "Video games have almost completely disappeared", "Board games are now used mainly in schools", "Modern board games depend entirely on luck"], 0, "The text describes growing sales and argues that \"the social side may be even more important\"."),
                new("What is unusual about some modern board games?", ["Players must use a smartphone to play", "The winner is always decided by dice", "Players work together against the game instead of competing", "Only two people are allowed to play"], 2, "Paragraph 2 says \"players do not compete at all but work together against the game\"."),
                new("In the last paragraph, what does \"revival\" mean?", ["A fall in sales", "A return to popularity", "A new kind of screen", "A difficult rule"], 1, "The whole text describes board games becoming popular again after people expected them to disappear."),
                new("What does the writer suggest about shy students?", ["They never enjoy board games", "They prefer to study alone with a screen", "They are usually better at maths than other students", "They may communicate more easily during a game than in a normal lesson"], 3, "Students who \"would never volunteer an answer in class will happily explain their strategy during a game\"."),
