        new("r-b2-2", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Life Without a Phone for a Day",
            "Most of us check our phones dozens of times a day, often without noticing. So what happens when the phone is suddenly gone? A growing number of studies have tried to answer this question, and the results are more complicated than you might expect.\n\nIn the first few hours, many people experience real discomfort. Participants in experiments report reaching for their pockets, imagining that they hear notifications and feeling anxious that they are missing something important. Psychologists have even invented a word for this fear of being without a phone: \"nomophobia\".\n\nYet the discomfort usually fades surprisingly quickly. After a day or two, people often describe a sense of relief. They notice more of their surroundings, have longer conversations and find it easier to concentrate on a single task. Some say that time seems to pass more slowly, in a pleasant way.\n\nOn the other hand, a completely phone-free life is unrealistic for most of us. Phones are how we pay for things, find our way around and stay in touch with family and colleagues. For this reason, many researchers argue that the goal should not be to abandon technology, but to use it more deliberately.\n\nTheir practical suggestions are simple: turn off non-essential notifications, keep the phone out of sight while working or eating, and choose certain times of day to check messages rather than responding to every alert immediately. Small changes like these, they say, can bring many of the benefits of a digital detox without the inconvenience of disappearing from the connected world.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Phones should be banned for everyone", "Nomophobia is a serious illness that needs medicine", "People rarely notice when their phone is missing", "Being without a phone is uncomfortable at first but can bring real benefits"], 3, "The text describes early \"discomfort\" followed by \"a sense of relief\" and other benefits."),
                new("How do people often feel after a day or two without their phone?", ["More anxious than before", "Relieved", "Extremely bored", "Angry with their friends"], 1, "Paragraph 3 says: \"After a day or two, people often describe a sense of relief.\""),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"deliberately\" mean?", ["By accident", "Very quickly", "Carefully and with a clear purpose", "Only rarely"], 2, "Researchers want people to make conscious choices about technology instead of using it automatically."),
                new("Why do researchers probably suggest checking messages at set times?", ["To reduce constant interruptions without losing contact", "Because phones break if they are used too often", "Because messages only arrive at certain times", "To save money on phone bills"], 0, "The advice aims to keep the benefits of a detox \"without disappearing from the connected world\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-3", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Chocolate Bar Behind the Microwave",
            "Many important inventions were not planned; they happened because someone noticed something strange and asked why. One of the best examples sits in almost every kitchen today: the microwave oven.\n\nIn the 1940s, an American engineer named Percy Spencer was working with radar equipment. Radar used a device called a magnetron to produce microwaves, a type of energy used to detect aircraft. One day, while standing near an active magnetron, Spencer realised that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted. Other people had probably noticed similar effects before, but Spencer was curious enough to investigate.\n\nHe tried placing popcorn kernels near the device, and they began to pop. Then, according to a well-known story, he tried an egg, which exploded in a colleague's face. It was clear that microwaves could heat food very quickly by making the water molecules inside it vibrate. Spencer and his company quickly realised the commercial potential of the discovery and applied for a patent.\n\nThe first commercial microwave ovens, however, were far from the compact machines we know. They were nearly as tall as a person, weighed hundreds of kilograms and were extremely expensive, so only restaurants, ships and railway companies bought them. It took about another twenty years for smaller, cheaper models to appear in ordinary homes. Even then, many people were suspicious of the new machines and worried, wrongly, that food cooked in them might be unsafe.\n\nToday microwave ovens are so common that few people think about where they came from. Yet the story is a useful reminder that curiosity can turn a small accident, like a ruined snack, into a technology used by millions of people every day.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Radar was originally invented to cook food", "A curious engineer turned a small accident into a useful invention", "Chocolate melts easily in warm pockets", "Microwave ovens have always been small and cheap"], 1, "The text shows how Spencer investigated a melted chocolate bar, which led to the microwave oven."),
                new("Who bought the first commercial microwave ovens?", ["Restaurants, ships and railway companies", "Ordinary families", "Schools and hospitals", "Chocolate factories"], 0, "Paragraph 4 says \"only restaurants, ships and railway companies bought them\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"compact\" mean?", ["Very noisy", "Very expensive", "Old-fashioned", "Small and not taking up much space"], 3, "The first ovens are contrasted with today's machines: they were \"nearly as tall as a person\"."),
                new("What does the writer suggest about the other people who noticed similar effects?", ["They invented the microwave oven before Spencer", "They did not work with radar", "They did not investigate what had happened", "They were afraid of the magnetron"], 2, "The text says Spencer, unlike them, \"was curious enough to investigate\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-4", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Is a Four-Day Week the Future of Work?",
            "For more than a century, the five-day working week has been the standard in most countries. Recently, however, a number of companies have experimented with something that once sounded impossible: paying employees the same salary for working only four days.\n\nThe results of these trials have been encouraging. In many cases, companies reported that productivity stayed the same or even increased. Employees said they felt less stressed, slept better and had more time for family, exercise and hobbies. Because staff were happier, fewer of them left their jobs, which saved the companies the cost of recruiting and training replacements.\n\nHow is it possible to do the same work in less time? Supporters point out that a normal working day contains a lot of wasted time: long meetings, unnecessary emails and constant interruptions. When people know they have only four days, they tend to plan more carefully and cut out activities that add little value.\n\nNevertheless, the idea has its critics. A four-day week is much easier to organise in an office than in a hospital, a school or a shop that must stay open every day. Some employees also found that their four working days became longer and more intense, which partly cancelled out the benefit of the extra day off.\n\nFor now, the four-day week is unlikely to become universal overnight. But the experiments have encouraged many managers to ask a valuable question: are we measuring work by the hours people spend at their desks, or by what they actually achieve?",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["All companies have already moved to a four-day week", "Working fewer days always lowers productivity", "Four-day week trials show benefits, although the idea does not suit every job", "Hospitals are the best places to test shorter weeks"], 2, "The text describes \"encouraging\" results but also explains the critics' concerns."),
                new("Why did the companies save money on recruitment?", ["They paid lower salaries", "They stopped holding meetings", "They closed on Fridays", "Fewer employees left their jobs"], 3, "Paragraph 2 says \"fewer of them left their jobs, which saved the companies the cost of recruiting\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"cancelled out\" mean?", ["Made something stronger", "Removed or balanced the effect of something", "Added something extra", "Moved something to a later date"], 1, "Longer, more intense days reduced the advantage of having an extra day off."),
                new("What does the final question suggest the writer believes?", ["Results matter more than the number of hours worked", "People should work longer hours", "Managers should watch employees more closely", "Offices should get rid of desks"], 0, "The writer contrasts \"hours people spend at their desks\" with \"what they actually achieve\"."),
