        new("r-b2-5", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Why Cities Are Hotter Than the Countryside",
            "On a hot summer evening, the temperature in the centre of a large city can be several degrees higher than in the fields just outside it. Scientists call this the urban heat island effect, and as cities grow and summers become hotter, it is attracting more and more attention.\n\nThe main cause is the materials that cities are made of. Asphalt roads, concrete buildings and dark rooftops absorb large amounts of heat from the sun during the day and release it slowly at night. In the countryside, by contrast, plants and soil hold water, and when that water evaporates it cools the air, much as sweat cools our skin. In addition, cars, air conditioners and factories all produce extra heat, and tall buildings can block the wind that might otherwise carry it away.\n\nThe consequences are serious. Higher temperatures increase energy use, as people run air conditioners for longer. They also worsen air quality and can be dangerous for elderly people and young children, especially during heatwaves, when even the nights offer no relief. Hospitals often record more heat-related admissions during such periods, particularly among people who live alone.\n\nFortunately, there are practical solutions. Planting trees along streets provides shade and cools the surrounding air. Some cities are painting roofs white or light grey so that they reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it. Others are creating \"green roofs\" covered with plants, and replacing car parks with parks and fountains. Even a single line of trees along a busy road can lower the temperature that people actually feel by several degrees.\n\nNone of these measures can remove the problem entirely, but together they can make city life noticeably more comfortable and, in extreme heat, safer.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Cities are warmer than the countryside, but the effect can be reduced", "The countryside is becoming hotter than cities", "Air conditioners are the only solution to heat", "Trees make city streets more dangerous"], 0, "The text explains the causes of the urban heat island effect and then \"practical solutions\"."),
                new("Why are some cities painting roofs white or light grey?", ["To make buildings look modern", "To protect them from rain", "Because dark paint is expensive", "So that the roofs reflect sunlight"], 3, "Paragraph 4 says roofs are painted \"so that they reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what does \"evaporates\" mean?", ["Freezes into ice", "Falls as rain", "Changes from liquid into gas", "Becomes dirty"], 2, "The water leaves the plants and soil as vapour and cools the air, \"much as sweat cools our skin\"."),
                new("Why would a city park probably be cooler than a car park next to it?", ["Parks are always shaded by tall buildings", "Its plants and soil hold water that cools the air", "Car parks get more wind", "Cars are not allowed near parks"], 1, "Paragraph 2 explains that plants and soil hold water which cools the air when it evaporates, unlike asphalt."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-1", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Too Many Jams on the Shelf",
            "Walk into a large supermarket in search of a jar of jam and you may be confronted with dozens of varieties: different fruits, sugar-free versions, organic brands and jars in every imaginable size. Conventional economic thinking assumes that more choice is always better, since it increases the likelihood that each shopper will find exactly what they want. Yet a substantial body of psychological research suggests that the relationship between choice and satisfaction is rather less straightforward.\n\nIn one frequently cited experiment, shoppers were offered samples from either a small or a large selection of jams. The larger display attracted more attention, but those who saw the smaller range were considerably more likely to actually make a purchase. The researchers concluded that an abundance of options can overwhelm people, making the decision feel so burdensome that they postpone it altogether. Notably, the jams were all of similar quality, so the difference could not be explained by the products themselves.\n\nEven when people do choose, an extensive range can undermine their contentment afterwards. With so many alternatives available, it is easy to imagine that one of the rejected options might have been better, and this nagging doubt diminishes the pleasure of the item actually chosen. Psychologists also distinguish between \"maximisers\", who feel compelled to find the best possible option, and \"satisficers\", who settle for one that is good enough. Intriguingly, maximisers often make objectively better decisions yet report feeling less happy with them.\n\nIt would be an oversimplification, however, to conclude that choice is inherently harmful. Later studies have found that the effect depends heavily on context: people who know exactly what they are looking for are rarely troubled by large selections, and well-organised displays that group options into clear categories appear to reduce the burden considerably. The lesson for everyday life may be modest but useful. For decisions that genuinely matter, careful comparison is worthwhile; for trivial ones, deliberately adopting the satisficer's mindset may save both time and a considerable amount of unnecessary regret.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Supermarkets should sell only one kind of jam", "Maximisers are generally happier than satisficers", "Economists have proved that choice is always harmful", "Having more options does not always lead to greater satisfaction"], 3, "The text argues that \"the relationship between choice and satisfaction is rather less straightforward\" than economists assume."),
                new("What happened in the jam experiment?", ["The larger display attracted less attention", "Nobody bought any jam at all", "Shoppers who saw fewer jams were more likely to buy one", "Shoppers mostly chose sugar-free jam"], 2, "Paragraph 2 says those who saw the smaller range \"were considerably more likely to actually make a purchase\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"nagging doubt\" mean?", ["A persistent feeling of uncertainty", "A strong sense of confidence", "A loud complaint", "A sudden decision"], 0, "It describes the continuing worry that \"one of the rejected options might have been better\"."),
                new("For which decision would the writer most likely recommend a satisficer's mindset?", ["Choosing which university to attend", "Choosing a brand of toothpaste", "Choosing a house to buy", "Choosing a surgeon for an operation"], 1, "The writer advises the satisficer's mindset for \"trivial\" decisions and careful comparison for those that \"genuinely matter\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-2", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "How Paper Travelled West Through Samarkand",
            "It is difficult to overstate the importance of paper. Before its arrival, scholars in western Asia and Europe wrote on papyrus, which was fragile and came mainly from Egypt, or on parchment, made from carefully prepared animal skins, which was durable but prohibitively expensive. A single large book could require the skins of an entire flock of sheep.\n\nPaper had been invented in China many centuries earlier, and for a long time the technique remained largely confined to East Asia. According to a widely repeated account, the knowledge reached Central Asia in the middle of the eighth century, when Chinese papermakers were taken prisoner after a battle near the Talas River and brought to Samarkand. Historians now treat this story with some caution, suspecting that the craft spread more gradually along trade routes. Whatever the precise route, Samarkand soon became renowned for its paper, which was smooth, pale and made largely from linen rags and plant fibres. Local craftsmen adapted the technique to the resources available to them, and travellers often praised their product for its quality.\n\nFrom Samarkand, papermaking spread to Baghdad, where a paper mill was reportedly established by the end of the eighth century, and then onwards to Damascus, Cairo and eventually Spain and Italy. The consequences were profound. Because paper was far cheaper than parchment, books became more affordable, libraries expanded and administrators could keep far more extensive records. Merchants benefited too, since contracts, letters of credit and accounts could now be written down cheaply and carried over long distances. The flourishing of science, mathematics and literature in cities such as Baghdad owed a great deal to this humble material.\n\nWhen we consider the technologies that transformed the circulation of knowledge, we tend to think first of the printing press or the internet. Yet neither would have had the same impact without the quiet revolution that preceded them, one that owes a considerable debt to the workshops of Samarkand. It is a fitting reminder that the most influential innovations are not always the most dramatic ones.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Paper was first invented in Samarkand", "The spread of papermaking through Samarkand had a major effect on learning", "Parchment was cheaper and more practical than paper", "The printing press mattered more than paper"], 1, "The text traces paper's route through Samarkand and says its consequences \"were profound\"."),
                new("What was Samarkand paper mainly made from?", ["Animal skins", "Papyrus from Egypt", "Silk and cotton", "Linen rags and plant fibres"], 3, "Paragraph 2 says it was \"made largely from linen rags and plant fibres\"."),
                new("In paragraph 1, what does \"prohibitively expensive\" mean?", ["So costly that most people could not afford it", "Only slightly more expensive than usual", "Cheap but low in quality", "Free for scholars"], 0, "Parchment was so costly that one book \"could require the skins of an entire flock of sheep\"."),
                new("What can be inferred about books before paper arrived?", ["Most ordinary families owned several of them", "Libraries were larger than they are today", "They were scarce because writing materials were fragile or costly", "Scholars hardly ever wrote anything down"], 2, "Papyrus was \"fragile\" and parchment \"prohibitively expensive\", and only with paper did books become \"more affordable\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-3", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Great Breakfast Debate",
            "\"Breakfast is the most important meal of the day\" is one of those phrases so often repeated that it has come to sound like established fact. Yet among nutrition scientists, the question of whether breakfast genuinely matters has proved surprisingly contentious.\n\nFor decades, the case for breakfast rested largely on observational studies, which consistently found that people who ate breakfast tended to be slimmer and healthier than those who skipped it. The problem with such studies is that they reveal correlation rather than causation. Regular breakfast eaters may also be more likely to exercise, sleep well and avoid smoking; it is almost impossible to separate the effect of the meal from the lifestyle that accompanies it. Moreover, people who know they are being studied may report their eating habits inaccurately, often in ways that flatter them.\n\nWhen researchers turned to randomised controlled trials, in which participants are assigned to eat or skip breakfast, the picture became considerably murkier. Several trials found that skipping breakfast did not lead to weight gain, and some suggested that people who ate breakfast simply consumed more calories over the day. Others found small benefits for particular groups, but the effects were modest and not always consistent. Interestingly, part of the enthusiasm for breakfast may have commercial roots: cereal manufacturers have long had an obvious interest in promoting the morning meal.\n\nNone of this means that breakfast is harmful. For children, some evidence suggests that a nutritious breakfast can support concentration at school, and many adults find that eating in the morning helps them avoid unhealthy snacking later. Athletes and people with certain medical conditions may also have specific needs.\n\nThe most defensible conclusion, therefore, is a rather unexciting one: what matters is less the timing of a meal than the overall quality and quantity of what we eat. For most healthy adults, the decision to eat breakfast can reasonably be guided by appetite and routine rather than by a slogan.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Breakfast is harmful for most adults", "Cereal is the healthiest possible breakfast", "The evidence that breakfast is essential for everyone is weaker than often believed", "Children should avoid eating breakfast"], 2, "The text shows the question is \"surprisingly contentious\" and concludes that the timing of meals matters less than their quality."),
                new("What was the main weakness of the early observational studies?", ["They showed correlation but not causation", "They studied only athletes", "They were paid for by doctors", "They included too few adults"], 0, "Paragraph 2 says such studies \"reveal correlation rather than causation\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"murkier\" mean?", ["Clearer and simpler", "More exciting", "More expensive", "Less clear and more confusing"], 3, "The trials produced mixed results that made the earlier simple picture harder to interpret."),
                new("Why does the writer mention cereal manufacturers?", ["To recommend a particular brand of cereal", "To suggest the popular belief may partly reflect business interests", "To prove that cereal causes weight gain", "To explain how cereal is produced"], 1, "The writer says the enthusiasm \"may have commercial roots\" because manufacturers benefit from promoting breakfast."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-4", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The True Price of a Cheap T-Shirt",
            "Clothing has never been cheaper. In many countries, a T-shirt can now be bought for less than the price of a sandwich, and fashion retailers introduce new collections not twice a year, as was once traditional, but almost every week. This model, commonly known as fast fashion, has made stylish clothing accessible to millions of people. Its hidden costs, however, are becoming increasingly difficult to ignore.\n\nThe environmental footprint of the industry is considerable. Growing conventional cotton requires vast quantities of water, while synthetic fabrics such as polyester are derived from oil and shed tiny plastic fibres every time they are washed, many of which end up in rivers and oceans. Because garments are designed to be fashionable for a single season, they are frequently discarded after only a handful of wears. A large proportion ends up in landfill or is burned, and only a small fraction of textiles is recycled into new clothing.\n\nThere is also a human dimension. Keeping prices low puts relentless pressure on the factories that produce the clothes, where workers may face long hours, low pay and unsafe conditions. Such realities are rarely visible to shoppers at the end of the supply chain, who see only an attractive price tag.\n\nEncouragingly, alternatives are gaining ground. Second-hand clothing, once associated with necessity rather than choice, has become fashionable in its own right, boosted by apps that allow people to resell garments with ease. Some brands now offer repair services or take back worn items for recycling, and a growing number of consumers are embracing the idea of a \"capsule wardrobe\": a small collection of versatile, durable pieces that can be combined in many ways.\n\nUltimately, the most sustainable garment is often the one already hanging in our wardrobe. Buying less, choosing well and wearing things for longer may not be glamorous advice, but it is arguably the most effective response to an industry built on constant novelty.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Fast fashion is cheap for shoppers but has significant hidden costs", "Cotton is always better for the environment than polyester", "Second-hand clothes are usually of poor quality", "Brands should release new collections more often"], 0, "The text states that fast fashion's \"hidden costs\" are \"becoming increasingly difficult to ignore\"."),
                new("What happens to polyester clothes when they are washed?", ["They shrink quickly", "They lose their colour", "They shed tiny plastic fibres", "They become stronger"], 2, "Paragraph 2 says synthetic fabrics \"shed tiny plastic fibres every time they are washed\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"versatile\" mean?", ["Very expensive", "Useful in many different ways", "Easily damaged", "Suitable only for special occasions"], 1, "The pieces are described as ones \"that can be combined in many ways\"."),
                new("What does the writer imply about second-hand clothing in the past?", ["It was more fashionable than new clothing", "It was sold mostly through apps", "It was produced in the same factories as fast fashion", "People mainly bought it because they could not afford new clothes"], 3, "The text says it was \"once associated with necessity rather than choice\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-5", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Adult Language Learning: Beyond the Myths",
            "It is widely assumed that anyone who begins learning a foreign language after childhood is doomed to fall short. Children, the argument goes, absorb languages effortlessly, while adults struggle with grammar tables and never lose their accent. Like many popular beliefs, this one contains a grain of truth wrapped in a good deal of exaggeration.\n\nThe grain of truth concerns pronunciation. Research consistently indicates that people who start learning a language in early childhood are far more likely to sound like native speakers. There is also evidence that, after many years of immersion, early starters tend to reach higher levels of ultimate attainment in grammar. However, these advantages typically emerge only after a very long period of living in the language.\n\nWhat is often overlooked, however, is that adults typically learn faster, at least in the early stages. They bring a mature understanding of how language works, well-developed memory strategies and, frequently, a clear motivation, whether it is a job, a move abroad or simple curiosity. In classroom settings with limited hours, adults and teenagers often outpace young children, who benefit most from years of constant, natural exposure rather than a few lessons a week. Older learners can also make deliberate use of dictionaries, grammar explanations and recordings, tools that young children cannot yet exploit.\n\nMoreover, a native-like accent is rarely the goal that matters most. The ability to hold a conversation, read a newspaper or negotiate a contract depends on vocabulary, fluency and cultural awareness, all of which adults can develop to a very high level. Plenty of respected writers and scientists acquired their working language as adults and kept a noticeable accent throughout their lives, without it diminishing their achievements in the slightest.\n\nPerhaps the most damaging aspect of the myth is that it becomes self-fulfilling: adults who believe they are too old tend to give up at the first difficulty. The evidence suggests they would be better served by consistent practice, realistic expectations and a little patience with themselves.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Only children can learn a foreign language well", "A native-like accent is the most important goal", "Adults should not start a new language after thirty", "Adults can learn languages very successfully, even though children have some advantages"], 3, "The writer says the popular belief \"contains a grain of truth wrapped in a good deal of exaggeration\" and shows adults' strengths."),
                new("In which area do early starters clearly have an advantage?", ["Vocabulary", "Pronunciation", "Motivation", "Memory strategies"], 1, "Paragraph 2 says \"the grain of truth concerns pronunciation\"."),
                new("In the last paragraph, what does \"self-fulfilling\" mean?", ["Impossible to prove", "Personally satisfying", "Becoming true because people believe it", "Popular among teachers"], 2, "Adults who believe they are too old give up, so the belief itself causes the failure it predicts."),
                new("What would the writer most likely say to a forty-year-old beginner?", ["Practise regularly and do not give up because of your age", "Focus mainly on losing your accent", "Do not start unless you can live abroad for years", "Wait until your children can learn with you"], 0, "The writer recommends \"consistent practice, realistic expectations and a little patience\"."),
            ])),
