        new("r-b1-6", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Life Before the Refrigerator",
            "Today most homes have a fridge, and we rarely think about it. For most of history, however, keeping food fresh was a daily problem. In hot weather, milk could go sour in a few hours, and meat could only be kept for long if it was dried, salted or smoked.\n\nPeople found clever solutions. In parts of Iran and Central Asia, builders made ice houses: deep buildings with very thick walls, where ice collected in winter could last well into the summer. In many villages, families dug cellars under their homes, because the temperature there stayed low all year. Others kept jars of milk and butter in cold streams.\n\nIn the nineteenth century, ice became a business. Workers cut huge blocks from frozen lakes, and ships carried them to hot cities thousands of kilometres away. Wealthier families bought an \"icebox\", a wooden cupboard with a block of ice inside, and a delivery man brought a new block every few days.\n\nThe electric refrigerator became common in homes only in the twentieth century, and in many countries not until after the 1950s. It changed more than the kitchen. People could shop less often, less food was thrown away, and new products, such as frozen vegetables, appeared in the shops.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["How people kept food fresh before and after the fridge", "Why ice houses are still used today", "How to make frozen vegetables at home", "The life of the man who invented the fridge"], 0, "The text describes old ways of keeping food cold and how the electric fridge changed things."),
                new("What was an \"icebox\"?", ["A building with very thick walls", "A room under a house", "A wooden cupboard with a block of ice inside", "A ship that carried ice"], 2, "Paragraph 3 explains that an icebox was \"a wooden cupboard with a block of ice inside\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what are \"cellars\"?", ["Shops that sell ice", "Rooms under a house", "Small streams near a village", "Large jars for milk"], 1, "Families \"dug\" them \"under their homes\", where it stayed cool."),
                new("Why did a delivery man bring a new block of ice every few days?", ["Because the ice became too cold", "Because families used ice for drinks only", "Because the law said so", "Because the old block slowly melted"], 3, "An icebox had no electricity, so the ice inside would slowly turn to water and need replacing."),
            ])),
        new("r-b1-7", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Tuman, the Dog Who Was Afraid",
            "Sardor is nineteen and studies biology at university. Every Saturday morning, instead of sleeping late, he takes a bus to an animal shelter on the edge of the city. The shelter looks after about sixty dogs and cats that have been lost or abandoned by their owners.\n\nThe work is not glamorous. Sardor cleans cages, washes bowls and takes the dogs for walks in the field next to the shelter. But his favourite job is spending time with the shy animals. Many of them have had a hard life and are afraid of people, and they need to learn that humans can be kind.\n\nLast winter a thin grey dog arrived. The staff called him Tuman, which means \"fog\" in Uzbek. For weeks he hid in the corner of his cage and would not take food from anyone's hand. Sardor sat near him every Saturday and read a book out loud, without looking at him. After a month, Tuman came closer. After two months, he let Sardor touch his head.\n\nIn spring a young couple came to the shelter and adopted Tuman. Sardor was sad to say goodbye, but he says it was the best day of the year. The experience has also helped him decide on his future: he now plans to become a vet.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["A young couple opens a shelter for dogs", "A biology student writes a book about dogs", "Why the city needs more animal shelters", "A student volunteers at a shelter and helps a frightened dog"], 3, "The text describes Sardor's Saturday work and his patient work with the dog Tuman."),
                new("What did Sardor do to help Tuman?", ["He fed him by hand every day", "He sat near him and read a book out loud", "He took him home for the winter", "He gave him a new name"], 1, "Paragraph 3 says Sardor \"sat near him every Saturday and read a book out loud\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"adopted\" mean?", ["Visited an animal for a short time", "Paid for an animal's food", "Took an animal home to become its new owners", "Gave an animal back to the shelter"], 2, "The couple took Tuman, so Sardor had to \"say goodbye\" to him."),
                new("Why was the day Tuman left \"the best day of the year\" for Sardor?", ["Because the dog he had helped finally found a home", "Because he no longer had to work on Saturdays", "Because he did not like Tuman", "Because the shelter gave him a prize"], 0, "Although he was sad, Sardor was happy that his patient work helped Tuman get a family."),
            ])),
        new("r-b1-8", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Wild Tulips of Central Asia",
            "When people think of tulips, they often imagine the colourful fields of the Netherlands. However, tulips did not begin their story in Europe. Their wild ancestors come from the mountains and dry grasslands of Central Asia, where dozens of different species still grow today.\n\nWild tulips look quite different from the ones sold in flower shops. Most of them are short, with small, bright red or yellow flowers, and they bloom for only a few weeks in early spring. Then the flowers and leaves die, and the plant survives the hot, dry summer as a bulb deep underground. When the rain returns the next spring, it grows again.\n\nOver the centuries, travellers and traders carried tulip bulbs west. The flowers became very fashionable, and by the seventeenth century some rare tulip bulbs in the Netherlands were sold for extraordinary prices. Gardeners there created thousands of new varieties from the original wild plants.\n\nToday, some wild tulips in Central Asia are becoming rare. Picking flowers, grazing animals and new roads and buildings all put them under pressure. In several places, the most important areas are now protected, and visitors are asked to take photographs rather than bunches of flowers.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["How to grow tulips in a flower shop", "Where tulips originally come from and why wild ones need protection", "Why tulips are expensive in the Netherlands today", "The best places in Europe to see flowers"], 1, "The text explains that tulips come from Central Asia and that some wild species are now rare."),
                new("How do wild tulips survive the summer?", ["As a bulb deep underground", "By growing taller leaves", "By flowering again in July", "By moving to higher mountains"], 0, "Paragraph 2 says the plant \"survives the hot, dry summer as a bulb deep underground\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"put them under pressure\" mean?", ["Help them to grow faster", "Make them more colourful", "Protect them from the rain", "Make it harder for them to survive"], 3, "Picking, grazing and building are listed as reasons why the tulips are \"becoming rare\"."),
                new("What can we guess about the tulips in modern flower shops?", ["They first grew in the Netherlands thousands of years ago", "They are exactly the same as wild tulips", "They were developed from wild Central Asian plants", "They only bloom for a few days"], 2, "Paragraph 3 says gardeners \"created thousands of new varieties from the original wild plants\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b1-9", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Selling Hand-Knitted Hats Online",
            "Two years ago, Gulnora knitted a warm woollen hat for her niece. A friend saw a photo of it and asked for one too, then another friend did the same. That was how Gulnora, a primary school teacher, started a small business from her living room.\n\nAt first she only sold to people she knew. Then her daughter helped her open a page on a social media site, and orders began to arrive from other cities. Most new customers heard about her from satisfied buyers, so Gulnora never paid for advertising. \"Word of mouth is still the best advertisement,\" she says.\n\nHer biggest early mistake was the price. She charged only the cost of the wool, because she thought her time was free. Soon she was knitting every evening and earning almost nothing. A friend who worked in a bank showed her how to calculate a fair price: the materials, the delivery, and a small payment for each hour of work. Some customers complained when her prices went up, but most stayed.\n\nToday Gulnora sells around thirty hats a month in autumn and winter. She is still a teacher and has no plans to leave her job. For her, the business is a way to earn extra money while doing something she loves.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["Why teachers should leave their jobs", "How to knit a woollen hat", "How a teacher turned her knitting into a small business", "A bank that helps small businesses"], 2, "The text follows Gulnora from her first hat to selling about thirty hats a month."),
                new("What was Gulnora's biggest early mistake?", ["She paid too much for advertising", "She bought the wrong kind of wool", "She sold hats only in summer", "She charged too little for her hats"], 3, "Paragraph 3 says she \"charged only the cost of the wool\" and earned \"almost nothing\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what does \"word of mouth\" mean?", ["Advertising on television", "People telling other people about something", "Writing reviews in a newspaper", "Talking to customers on the phone"], 1, "New customers \"heard about her from satisfied buyers\", not from paid adverts."),
                new("What can we guess about most of Gulnora's customers?", ["They think her hats are worth the higher price", "They stopped buying after the price went up", "They are all her friends and family", "They live in the same street as her"], 0, "When prices went up, \"most stayed\", which suggests they value the hats."),
            ])),
        new("r-b1-10", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Why Do We Yawn?",
            "Everybody yawns. Babies yawn before they are even born, and so do many animals, including dogs, cats, lions and some birds. Yet scientists still do not fully agree on why we do it.\n\nFor a long time, the most popular explanation was that we yawn because our bodies need more oxygen. It sounds logical, but experiments have not supported it. In one study, people breathed air with extra oxygen, and they yawned just as often as before.\n\nA newer idea is that yawning helps to cool the brain. When we take a deep breath, cool air enters the body and blood flow in the head increases. Some researchers have found that people yawn more often when the air is cooler than their body, and less on very hot days. This might explain why we yawn when we are tired or bored, because the brain may be slightly warmer at these times.\n\nThe strangest thing about yawning is that it is contagious. Seeing someone yawn, hearing a yawn or even reading about yawning can make us do it too. Interestingly, studies suggest that we are more likely to \"catch\" a yawn from family and close friends than from strangers. So if you have yawned while reading this, you are not alone.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["Different explanations for why people yawn", "How to stop yawning in class", "Why animals sleep more than humans", "The dangers of breathing cold air"], 0, "The text discusses the oxygen idea, the brain-cooling idea and contagious yawning."),
                new("What happened when people breathed air with extra oxygen?", ["They stopped yawning completely", "They yawned much more often", "They felt very tired", "They yawned as often as before"], 3, "Paragraph 2 says \"they yawned just as often as before\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"contagious\" mean?", ["Very dangerous to health", "Only happening at night", "Easily passed from one person to another", "Difficult to notice"], 2, "Seeing or hearing someone yawn can make us yawn too, so it spreads between people."),
                new("What can we guess from the last paragraph?", ["People never yawn when they are alone", "Reading about yawning can make readers yawn", "Strangers make us yawn more than friends", "Writers want their readers to fall asleep"], 1, "The writer says reading about yawning can cause it, then jokes \"if you have yawned while reading this, you are not alone\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b1-11", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Art Under the Streets of Tashkent",
            "Many visitors to Tashkent say that one of the most beautiful places in the city is not a palace or a park, but its underground railway. The Tashkent Metro opened in 1977 and was the first metro in Central Asia. Today it carries a large number of commuters to work and school every day.\n\nWhat makes the metro special is that almost every station has its own design. Some have huge glass chandeliers, others have walls covered with marble or with hand-painted ceramic tiles. At Kosmonavtlar station, for example, dark blue walls and round pictures of space travellers make passengers feel as if they are travelling through space.\n\nThe metro also had to be strong. In 1966, a powerful earthquake badly damaged the city, so the tunnels and stations were designed to be especially resistant to earthquakes.\n\nFor many years, taking photographs in the metro was not allowed. When this rule ended in 2018, pictures of the stations quickly spread on social media, and the metro became a popular attraction for tourists. Many visitors now buy a ticket just to ride from station to station and admire the decoration, although local people usually walk past it quickly on their way to work.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Tashkent's metro is the largest in the world", "Taking photographs in public places is dangerous", "The 1966 earthquake destroyed the metro", "Tashkent's metro is famous for its beautiful and varied stations"], 3, "The text focuses on the special design of the stations and why tourists visit them."),
                new("When did the metro open?", ["In 1966", "In 2018", "In 1977", "In 1957"], 2, "Paragraph 1 says: \"The Tashkent Metro opened in 1977.\""),
                new("In paragraph 1, what are \"commuters\"?", ["People who travel regularly to work or school", "Tourists who visit museums", "Workers who build tunnels", "Artists who design stations"], 0, "The metro carries them \"to work and school every day\"."),
                new("What can we guess about the change in 2018?", ["It made the metro less popular", "It helped more tourists learn about the metro", "It stopped local people from using the metro", "It closed some of the stations"], 1, "After photos were allowed, pictures \"spread on social media\" and the metro became a tourist attraction."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-6", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "What Makes Sourdough Rise",
            "Long before packets of dried yeast appeared in shops, bakers made bread rise using nothing more than flour, water and patience. The result, known as sourdough, has become fashionable again, and it is worth understanding what is actually happening inside the dough.\n\nEverything begins with a \"starter\". When flour and water are mixed and left in a warm place, wild yeasts and bacteria that live naturally on the grain begin to multiply. The yeasts produce bubbles of carbon dioxide gas, which become trapped in the dough and make it rise. Meanwhile, the bacteria produce acids, which give the bread its slightly sour taste and help it stay fresh for longer. To keep a starter alive, the baker has to feed it regularly with fresh flour and water, rather like looking after a pet. Some bakeries claim that their starters have been kept going for decades.\n\nCommercial yeast, by contrast, usually contains a single fast-working type of yeast. It can make a loaf rise in an hour or two, which is ideal for factories and busy home cooks. Sourdough is far slower: the dough is often left for many hours, sometimes overnight, before baking. Supporters argue that this long, slow process creates a deeper flavour and a chewier texture.\n\nSome people also say that they find sourdough easier to digest, perhaps because the bacteria break down part of the flour during the long rise. Scientists are still studying this question, however, and the evidence so far is limited. What is certain is that each starter is slightly different, which is why no two bakeries' sourdough tastes exactly the same.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["Why factories no longer use commercial yeast", "How sourdough bread is made to rise and how it differs from other bread", "The health dangers of eating bread", "How to open a successful bakery"], 1, "The text explains starters, compares them with commercial yeast and discusses sourdough's qualities."),
                new("What gives sourdough its slightly sour taste?", ["Carbon dioxide produced by yeast", "Extra salt added by bakers", "Baking the bread overnight", "Acids produced by bacteria"], 3, "Paragraph 2 says the bacteria \"produce acids, which give the bread its slightly sour taste\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what does it mean to \"feed\" a starter?", ["To add fresh flour and water to keep it active", "To give it to animals", "To bake it in the oven", "To add sugar to make it sweeter"], 0, "The text says the baker must \"feed it regularly with fresh flour and water\"."),
                new("What can we infer about the claim that sourdough is easier to digest?", ["Scientists have shown that it is false", "All scientists agree that it is true", "It may be true but has not yet been clearly proven", "It only applies to bread made in factories"], 2, "The writer says scientists are \"still studying this question\" and the evidence is \"limited\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-7", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Homework Question",
            "Few topics cause as much disagreement between parents, teachers and students as homework. Some parents complain that their children spend whole evenings on worksheets, while others worry that schools do not set enough. So what does the research actually say?\n\nThe honest answer is that it depends on age. Studies suggest that for secondary school students, a reasonable amount of homework is linked to better results, especially when it gives them practice in skills they have already learned in class. For younger children, however, the connection is much weaker. Beyond a certain point, more homework seems to bring diminishing returns: an extra hour of exercises produces little improvement, while it takes time away from sleep, play and family life.\n\nThe type of task also matters. Copying definitions or completing long lists of similar exercises may keep students busy without teaching them much. Reading for pleasure, preparing for a class discussion or working on a small project are often more valuable, because they require thinking rather than repetition.\n\nThere is also a question of fairness. A student with a quiet room, a computer and parents who can help has a clear advantage over one who shares a small flat with several siblings or works in the evenings. For this reason, some schools now offer supervised homework clubs after lessons, where every student has the same conditions.\n\nPerhaps, then, the real question is not whether homework should exist, but what it should look like. Short, purposeful tasks that students can manage on their own appear to be far more useful than hours of busy work.",
            [
                new("What is the writer's main point?", ["Homework should be banned in all schools", "Students should do at least three hours of homework a day", "The value of homework depends on students' age and the kind of task", "Parents should do homework with their children"], 2, "The writer says the answer \"depends on age\" and that \"the type of task also matters\"."),
                new("What do some schools offer to make homework fairer?", ["Supervised homework clubs after lessons", "Free computers for every family", "Extra homework for younger children", "Lessons for parents in the evening"], 0, "Paragraph 4 mentions \"supervised homework clubs after lessons, where every student has the same conditions\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what does \"diminishing returns\" mean?", ["Students return their homework late", "Results become much better very quickly", "Teachers give back fewer marks", "Each extra amount of effort brings less and less benefit"], 3, "The text explains that \"an extra hour of exercises produces little improvement\"."),
                new("What would the writer probably think of a task asking students to copy fifty definitions?", ["It is the most useful kind of homework", "It keeps students busy but teaches them little", "It is only suitable for secondary students", "It is fairer than a small project"], 1, "Paragraph 3 says copying definitions \"may keep students busy without teaching them much\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-8", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Surprising Mind of the Octopus",
            "If you wanted to find an intelligent animal very different from ourselves, you could hardly do better than the octopus. It has no bones, three hearts and blue blood, and yet it regularly surprises the scientists who study it.\n\nOctopuses in aquariums have been seen unscrewing the lids of jars to reach food inside, and some have famously escaped from their tanks at night. In the wild, one species collects empty coconut shells, carries them across the sea floor and later hides inside them for protection. Laboratory studies also suggest that octopuses can recognise individual people, behaving calmly with some keepers and squirting water at others.\n\nPart of the explanation lies in their unusual nervous system. An octopus has around five hundred million neurons, but roughly two-thirds of them are located in its arms rather than in its brain. Each arm can therefore taste, touch and react to the world with a surprising degree of independence, while the brain gives more general instructions.\n\nWhat makes this intelligence especially interesting is that it developed completely separately from our own. The last common ancestor of humans and octopuses was probably a simple, worm-like creature that lived more than five hundred million years ago. Intelligence, it seems, has evolved more than once on Earth, along very different paths.\n\nThere is also a puzzle. Many clever animals, such as dolphins and chimpanzees, live long lives in social groups, where learning from others is useful. Most octopuses, by contrast, are solitary and live for only one or two years. Why such a short-lived animal should need such a powerful mind remains an open question.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["The unusual intelligence of the octopus and questions it raises", "How to keep an octopus in an aquarium", "Why octopuses live in large social groups", "The history of dolphins and chimpanzees"], 0, "The text describes examples of octopus intelligence, its nervous system and the puzzle of its short life."),
                new("Where are most of an octopus's neurons?", ["In its brain", "In its three hearts", "In its arms", "In its blood"], 2, "Paragraph 3 says \"roughly two-thirds of them are located in its arms\"."),
                new("In paragraph 5, what does \"solitary\" mean?", ["Living for a very long time", "Living alone rather than in groups", "Very dangerous to other animals", "Active only at night"], 1, "Octopuses are contrasted with animals that \"live long lives in social groups\"."),
                new("What can we infer from the fact that octopuses squirt water at some keepers?", ["They are always aggressive towards humans", "They are trying to escape from their tanks", "They cannot see very well", "They can tell different people apart"], 3, "The text links this behaviour to studies suggesting octopuses \"can recognise individual people\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-9", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Frozen Water Towers of Central Asia",
            "High in the Pamir and Tien Shan mountains lie thousands of glaciers, huge rivers of ice that move slowly downhill. They are rarely seen by the millions of people living in the plains below, yet those people depend on them every day. For this reason, scientists often call glaciers \"water towers\".\n\nThe system works like a natural bank account. In winter, snow falls on the mountains and gradually turns into ice. In summer, when temperatures rise and there is little rain in the lowlands, part of this ice melts. The meltwater feeds great rivers such as the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, which supply water for drinking, farming and electricity just when it is needed most. Without this summer flow, many fields in the valleys below would dry up during the hottest months.\n\nIn recent decades, however, many of the region's glaciers have been getting smaller as average temperatures have risen. At first, this can create a misleading picture. As more ice melts, rivers may actually carry more water than before. But this cannot last. Once a glacier has shrunk beyond a certain point, the amount of meltwater begins to fall, and it may continue to fall for many years. Scientists call this moment \"peak water\".\n\nTo prepare, researchers measure glaciers using satellites as well as instruments placed directly on the ice. Meanwhile, farmers and planners are looking at ways to use water more carefully, such as drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to the roots of plants instead of flooding whole fields.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Glaciers are dangerous places for tourists", "Rivers in Central Asia are getting bigger every year", "Drip irrigation was invented in Central Asia", "Mountain glaciers are an important water source, and changes to them need planning"], 3, "The text explains how glaciers supply water, how they are shrinking and how people are preparing."),
                new("When do glaciers provide most of their water to the rivers?", ["In winter, when snow falls", "In summer, when there is little rain in the lowlands", "In spring, during heavy rain", "At night, when temperatures fall"], 1, "Paragraph 2 says that in summer \"part of this ice melts\" when \"there is little rain in the lowlands\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"misleading\" mean?", ["Very frightening", "Scientifically proven", "Giving a false idea of the situation", "Difficult to measure"], 2, "More water in rivers might look positive, but the writer explains that \"this cannot last\"."),
                new("Why does the writer compare glaciers to \"a natural bank account\"?", ["Water is saved as ice in winter and used in summer", "Glaciers help people earn money from tourism", "Banks pay for glacier research", "Glaciers grow larger every year like savings"], 0, "Snow is stored as ice in winter and \"spent\" as meltwater in summer, like saving and spending money."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-10", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Learning Everything from Video Tutorials",
            "Want to fix a dripping tap, play the guitar or bake the perfect loaf? A few years ago, you would probably have needed a teacher, a class or at least a good book. Today, millions of people simply search for a video tutorial, and within seconds a stranger on the other side of the world is showing them what to do.\n\nThe advantages are obvious. Most tutorials are free, and learners can watch them whenever they like. Unlike in a classroom, you can pause the video, repeat a difficult part as many times as necessary and skip what you already know. For practical skills in particular, seeing someone's hands actually do the task is often clearer than any written explanation.\n\nHowever, video learning has its weaknesses. The biggest is the lack of feedback. A video cannot see you, so it cannot tell you that you are holding the guitar wrongly or that your knife technique is unsafe. Mistakes that nobody corrects can easily become bad habits. The quality of tutorials also varies enormously, and it is not always easy for a beginner to tell a real expert from someone who simply presents confidently.\n\nThere is also a subtler trap. Watching someone else perform a skill can create a pleasant feeling that we are learning, even when we are only being entertained. Many people watch hours of cooking videos and still cook the same three dishes. Real progress, experts argue, comes from trying, failing and trying again.\n\nThe most effective learners therefore use videos as a starting point rather than a complete course. They practise straight away, record themselves, and look for someone, whether a friend or an online community, who can give them honest comments.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["How to become a famous video presenter", "The benefits and limits of learning skills from online videos", "Why books are better than teachers", "How to fix a dripping tap"], 1, "The text discusses advantages, weaknesses and the best way to use video tutorials."),
                new("According to the text, what is the biggest weakness of video learning?", ["Learners receive no feedback on their mistakes", "Videos are usually too expensive", "Videos are always too long", "Learners cannot pause the videos"], 0, "Paragraph 3 says: \"The biggest is the lack of feedback.\""),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"a subtler trap\" refer to?", ["A mistake that is very easy to notice", "A trick used to sell products", "A difficult test at the end of a course", "A less obvious danger that is easy to miss"], 3, "The writer describes the hidden problem of feeling we are learning while \"only being entertained\"."),
                new("What does the example of cooking videos suggest?", ["Cooking is the easiest skill to learn online", "People should only cook three dishes", "Watching alone does not lead to real improvement", "Cooking videos contain wrong information"], 2, "People watch \"hours of cooking videos\" but still cook the same dishes, because they do not practise."),
            ])),
        new("r-b2-11", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Why Losing Can Help Young Athletes",
            "Walk past any children's sports competition and you will see parents celebrating every goal as if it were a world championship final. It is natural to want children to win. Yet many coaches believe that losing, handled well, is one of the most valuable experiences a young athlete can have.\n\nOne reason is that early success can be misleading. In many sports, children who are born early in the selection year are more likely to be chosen for top teams, simply because they are older, taller and stronger than their teammates. They win a lot at ten or eleven, but this advantage often disappears as others catch up. Meanwhile, some late developers, who lost regularly as children, go on to become excellent adult players because they were forced to rely on skill and hard work.\n\nDefeat also teaches lessons that victory cannot. After a loss, young athletes have to deal with disappointment, analyse what went wrong and decide what to practise next. Coaches say this builds resilience, the ability to recover from difficulties, which is useful far beyond sport.\n\nOf course, losing only helps if adults respond in the right way. A child who is criticised or ignored after a defeat may simply decide that the sport is not for them. Experts recommend focusing on effort and improvement rather than the result: asking \"What did you learn today?\" instead of \"Why did you lose?\"\n\nNone of this means that winning is unimportant. But a young athlete who has never lost may not know what to do when, eventually, the defeats arrive.",
            [
                new("What is the writer's main argument?", ["Children should not take part in competitions", "Parents should celebrate every goal", "Losing, if handled well, can benefit young athletes", "Only the tallest children should play sport"], 2, "The first paragraph says losing \"handled well\" is one of the most valuable experiences."),
                new("Why are children born early in the selection year often chosen for top teams?", ["Because they practise more than others", "Because their parents pay extra", "Because they are better at analysing games", "Because they are older and physically bigger"], 3, "Paragraph 2 says it is \"simply because they are older, taller and stronger\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"resilience\" mean?", ["The ability to run very fast", "The ability to recover from difficulties", "A strong wish to win every game", "Fear of losing"], 1, "The text defines it directly as \"the ability to recover from difficulties\"."),
                new("What can we infer about the question \"Why did you lose?\"", ["It may make a child focus on the result rather than learning", "It is the best question a parent can ask", "Coaches always ask it after a win", "It helps children become more resilient"], 0, "Experts suggest asking \"What did you learn today?\" instead, to focus on effort and improvement."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-6", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Painless Purchase",
            "Handing over a banknote involves a small but genuine moment of discomfort. You see the money leave your hand, you count the change, and your wallet is visibly thinner afterwards. Behavioural economists call this sensation the \"pain of paying\", and they argue that it plays a surprisingly important role in keeping our spending under control.\n\nModern payment methods are designed, whether deliberately or not, to remove that pain. Tapping a card or a phone takes less than a second and leaves no physical trace. Online shops store our details so that a purchase requires a single click, and subscriptions renew automatically without any decision at all. Each of these innovations makes buying more convenient, but convenience comes at a price: when paying stops feeling like a loss, we are inclined to pay more, and more often.\n\nThere is some evidence for this. In one well-known experiment, participants who were told to pay by card were willing to offer considerably more for the same tickets than those who had to pay in cash. Other studies have found that people underestimate how much they have spent when they rarely handle physical money. The effect is not universal, and researchers disagree about its size, but the general pattern appears fairly consistent.\n\nNone of this means we should return to paying for everything in coins. Digital payments are faster, often safer, and leave a record that can be extremely useful. Indeed, the same technology that makes spending effortless can also restore some of its visibility. Many banking apps now send a notification after every purchase or divide spending into categories, reintroducing a little of the friction that cash once provided naturally.\n\nPerhaps the lesson is simply to notice when a system has been designed to make a decision feel weightless, and to add back, deliberately, a moment of thought.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Painless digital payments can encourage us to spend more than we realise", "Cash is always safer than digital payment", "Banking apps are the main cause of debt", "Economists want to ban subscriptions"], 0, "The text explains the \"pain of paying\" and how modern methods remove it, leading to more spending."),
                new("What did the well-known experiment find?", ["People paying in cash spent more on tickets", "People could not remember which payment method they used", "Card users bought fewer tickets overall", "People paying by card offered more for the same tickets"], 3, "Paragraph 3 says card payers \"were willing to offer considerably more for the same tickets\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"friction\" mean in this context?", ["A disagreement between banks and customers", "The physical wear on a bank card", "A small obstacle that makes us pause before spending", "A fee charged for each transaction"], 2, "Notifications reintroduce a little of the pause and awareness that cash \"once provided naturally\"."),
                new("What would the writer most likely recommend?", ["Cancelling all bank cards immediately", "Using app features that make spending more visible", "Avoiding online shops completely", "Paying for subscriptions only in cash"], 1, "The writer praises notifications and categories, and suggests adding \"a moment of thought\" rather than giving up digital payments."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-7", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Tiny Forests in Big Cities",
            "In school playgrounds, beside motorways and on forgotten corners of land, a new kind of forest is appearing. These so-called tiny forests may be no larger than a tennis court, yet their supporters claim that they can grow into dense, self-sustaining woodland within a few years.\n\nThe approach is usually traced back to the Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki, who spent decades studying the native vegetation of his country. His method differs sharply from conventional tree planting. Instead of a few well-spaced trees of the same species, volunteers plant a wide mix of local trees and shrubs very close together, often three or more per square metre, in soil that has been carefully prepared. The young plants compete for light, which pushes them to grow upwards quickly, and after two or three years of weeding and watering they are generally left to look after themselves.\n\nSome of the claims made for the method, however, deserve caution. Figures suggesting that such forests grow ten times faster or become thirty times denser than ordinary ones are frequently repeated, but independent studies are still limited, and results depend heavily on climate, soil and aftercare. Some projects have failed when planting was not followed by proper maintenance. Ecologists also point out that a patch of young trees, however lively, is no substitute for protecting large, mature forests, which store far more carbon and shelter far more species.\n\nEven so, tiny forests offer real benefits on a local scale. They provide shade on hot streets, attract birds and insects, and absorb rainwater that might otherwise flood roads. Perhaps just as importantly, they give city residents, and especially children, a direct connection with nature. Planting days typically involve whole neighbourhoods, and people who helped to plant a forest tend to feel responsible for it long afterwards. In that sense, the greatest value of a tiny forest may lie not only in the trees themselves but in the community that grows around them.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["Why large forests should be replaced with small ones", "The life story of a Japanese botanist", "How to plant a tree in a school playground", "The tiny forest method, its benefits and its limitations"], 3, "The text describes the method, questions some claims about it and lists its local benefits."),
                new("How is the tiny forest method different from conventional tree planting?", ["Only one type of tree is planted far apart", "The trees are never watered", "Many different local species are planted very close together", "Adult trees are brought from other countries"], 2, "Paragraph 2 contrasts \"a few well-spaced trees of the same species\" with \"a wide mix of local trees and shrubs very close together\"."),
                new("In paragraph 1, what does \"self-sustaining\" mean?", ["Able to survive without continued human help", "Growing only in the sun", "Needing daily care from volunteers", "Producing fruit to sell"], 0, "Paragraph 2 confirms that after two or three years the plants \"are generally left to look after themselves\"."),
                new("What is the writer's attitude towards the claim that tiny forests grow ten times faster?", ["Completely convinced by it", "Cautious, because there is little independent evidence", "Angry because it is obviously false", "Uninterested in whether it is true"], 1, "The writer says such claims \"deserve caution\" because \"independent studies are still limited\"."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-8", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Observatory That Measured the Year",
            "On a low hill on the edge of Samarkand, visitors descend a narrow staircase into a deep trench cut into the rock. Inside it lies a great curved stone track, the only substantial part of what was once one of the finest astronomical observatories in the world. It was built in the 1420s by Ulugh Beg, a grandson of Timur, who ruled Samarkand but was, by temperament, far more a scholar than a warrior.\n\nUlugh Beg gathered some of the leading mathematicians and astronomers of his time and gave them an instrument of extraordinary size. Its main feature was an enormous arc, with a radius of about forty metres, set exactly along the north–south line. Because the arc was so large, even a tiny movement of a star across the sky could be measured with remarkable precision, long before the invention of the telescope.\n\nThe results justified the effort. The observatory's astronomers produced a catalogue listing the positions of over a thousand stars, the most comprehensive such work for centuries. They also calculated the length of the year, relative to the stars, as 365 days, 6 hours, 10 minutes and 8 seconds, a figure that differs from modern measurements by less than a minute.\n\nAfter Ulugh Beg's death the observatory fell into disuse, and over time its buildings disappeared almost entirely, until even its location was uncertain. It was only in 1908 that the archaeologist Vasily Vyatkin, guided partly by old documents, uncovered the underground section of the arc. By then, however, the star catalogue had already reached Europe: a Latin translation was published in Oxford in the seventeenth century, where astronomers studied it with considerable respect.\n\nToday the site is a reminder that careful observation, patient calculation and generous support for learning can achieve astonishing results, even with instruments made of stone.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["The military campaigns of Timur", "A fifteenth-century observatory in Samarkand and its scientific achievements", "The invention of the telescope in Europe", "How modern astronomers measure the year"], 1, "The text describes Ulugh Beg's observatory, its huge instrument, its results and its rediscovery."),
                new("How accurate was the observatory's calculation of the length of the year?", ["It was wrong by about six hours", "It was exactly the same as today's figure", "It was never compared with modern measurements", "It differs from modern measurements by less than a minute"], 3, "Paragraph 3 says the figure \"differs from modern measurements by less than a minute\"."),
                new("In paragraph 4, what does \"fell into disuse\" mean?", ["Stopped being used", "Was destroyed by an earthquake", "Became more famous", "Was moved to a new place"], 0, "After this, the buildings \"disappeared almost entirely\", which suggests nobody was using or maintaining them."),
                new("Why was the size of the arc important?", ["A larger arc could be seen from far away", "A larger arc could be used as a staircase", "A larger arc made it possible to measure small movements more precisely", "A larger arc made the observatory cheaper to build"], 2, "Paragraph 2 says \"because the arc was so large, even a tiny movement of a star\" could be measured precisely."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-9", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Curious Power of the Placebo",
            "In medical trials, some patients receive the real treatment while others are given a placebo: a pill or procedure that looks identical but contains no active ingredient. The comparison is designed to show whether a new drug genuinely works. Yet for decades, researchers have noticed something awkward. Many patients in the placebo group improve too, sometimes considerably.\n\nIt would be easy to dismiss this as imagination, but the placebo effect has measurable physical causes. When people expect a treatment to relieve pain, the brain can release its own natural painkilling chemicals. In experiments where these chemicals were blocked by another drug, much of the placebo's effect on pain disappeared. Expectation, in other words, is not merely a feeling; it can change what happens in the body.\n\nThe details of a treatment matter surprisingly much. Studies have suggested that injections tend to produce stronger placebo effects than pills, that two pills can outperform one, and that a placebo presented as expensive may work better than one described as cheap. Even more surprisingly, some trials have found that patients improved when openly told they were taking an inert pill, provided it was offered with a clear explanation and a caring manner.\n\nThere are important limits, however. Placebos appear to influence symptoms such as pain, nausea and tiredness, which depend partly on how the brain interprets signals from the body. They do not shrink tumours, cure infections or repair broken bones, and relying on them instead of effective treatment can be dangerous. The effect also has a darker twin, sometimes called the nocebo effect: patients who are warned about possible side effects occasionally begin to experience them, even when they are taking nothing at all.\n\nFor doctors, the practical lesson may be less about sugar pills and more about communication, since the way a treatment is explained can itself shape how well it works.",
            [
                new("What is the main idea of the text?", ["Placebos can cure most serious diseases", "Medical trials no longer need placebo groups", "Expectations can produce real, though limited, effects on the body", "Expensive medicines are always more effective"], 2, "The text explains the physical basis of the placebo effect, what influences it and its limits."),
                new("What happened in experiments where the brain's natural painkillers were blocked?", ["Much of the placebo's effect on pain disappeared", "The placebo became much stronger", "Patients stopped feeling any pain", "The patients refused to continue"], 0, "Paragraph 2 says \"much of the placebo's effect on pain disappeared\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"inert\" mean?", ["Extremely expensive", "Dangerous to take", "Newly invented", "Having no active effect"], 3, "The patients were told they were taking a pill with no active ingredient, which is the meaning of an inert pill."),
                new("What can be inferred about how doctors describe side effects?", ["Doctors should never mention side effects", "The way they are described may influence what patients feel", "Side effects are always imaginary", "Patients ignore what doctors tell them"], 1, "The nocebo effect shows warnings can cause symptoms, and the conclusion stresses that explanation \"can itself shape\" results."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-10", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "In Defence of the Pen",
            "In many classrooms and lecture halls today, the sound of pens on paper has been replaced by the quiet clicking of keyboards. Typing is faster, notes can be searched and shared instantly, and nobody has to struggle to read their own handwriting. It seems obvious that the laptop is the superior tool. Yet a growing body of research suggests that the answer is less straightforward.\n\nThe central argument concerns what happens in the mind while we take notes. Most people can type almost as quickly as a lecturer speaks, which makes it tempting to record everything verbatim, like a stenographer. Writing by hand is much slower, so students are forced to listen, decide what matters and express it in their own words. This process of selection and summary is itself a form of learning. Several studies have found that students who took notes by hand understood the underlying ideas better, although attempts to repeat these experiments have produced mixed results, and the size of the advantage remains debated.\n\nEvidence from young children points in a similar direction. Some research suggests that children who practise forming letters by hand recognise them more easily than those who learn only by typing, possibly because the movement of the hand helps to fix the shape of each letter in memory.\n\nNone of this is an argument for abandoning technology. Laptops are invaluable for students with certain disabilities, for collaborative work and for tasks such as drafting long essays, where the ability to reorganise text is a real advantage. Nor is handwriting magic: a student who copies slides mechanically with a pen gains little.\n\nThe lesson, rather, is that tools shape the way we think. The best choice may depend less on which device is more modern than on which one encourages the kind of thinking a particular task requires.",
            [
                new("What is the writer's main point?", ["Handwriting can support learning, but the best tool depends on the task", "Laptops should be banned in classrooms", "Handwriting is always better than typing", "Children no longer need to learn to write by hand"], 0, "The writer presents evidence for handwriting but concludes that the choice depends on \"the kind of thinking a particular task requires\"."),
                new("According to the text, why might handwritten notes help understanding?", ["Handwritten notes are easier to share", "Students can write faster than they type", "Writing slowly forces students to select and summarise ideas", "Teachers prefer to read handwritten notes"], 2, "Paragraph 2 says students are \"forced to listen, decide what matters and express it in their own words\"."),
                new("In paragraph 2, what does \"verbatim\" mean?", ["In a short summary", "Using exactly the same words as were spoken", "In a foreign language", "Without any mistakes in spelling"], 1, "Typing fast makes it tempting to \"record everything\", like a stenographer who writes down every word."),
                new("What does the writer suggest about a student who mechanically copies slides with a pen?", ["The student will learn more than anyone else", "The student should switch to typing immediately", "The student is doing exactly what the research recommends", "The student benefits little because no real thinking is involved"], 3, "The writer says \"Nor is handwriting magic\" and such a student \"gains little\", showing that the thinking matters, not just the pen."),
            ])),
        new("r-c1-11", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "The Box That Shrank the World",
            "Almost everything in a typical home, from the phone in your pocket to the bananas in the kitchen, has probably spent part of its journey inside a plain steel box. The shipping container is so ordinary that it is easy to overlook, yet few inventions have done more to reshape the global economy.\n\nBefore containers, loading a ship was slow, costly and labour-intensive. Goods arrived at ports in sacks, barrels and crates of every shape and size, and teams of dockworkers had to carry or lift each item on board individually. A ship could spend a week or more in port being loaded and unloaded, and theft and damage were common. Transport costs were so high that, for many products, it simply made no sense to manufacture them far from where they would be sold.\n\nThe change is usually credited to Malcom McLean, an American trucking businessman. In 1956 he sent a converted tanker from Newark to Houston carrying fifty-eight metal boxes that could be lifted straight from lorries onto the ship. The idea was simple, but its full impact depended on something less glamorous: standardisation. Once international agreements fixed common sizes and fittings in the following decade, a container could move from ship to train to lorry anywhere in the world without its contents ever being touched.\n\nThe consequences were enormous. Loading times fell from days to hours, and the cost of moving goods across oceans dropped dramatically. Companies could now make components in one country, assemble them in another and sell the finished product in a third. Old city-centre ports declined, replaced by vast container terminals elsewhere, and the number of dockworkers needed fell sharply. Ships themselves grew ever larger, and today the biggest can carry more than twenty thousand standard containers.\n\nThe container did not create globalisation on its own, but it made the modern, closely connected world economy far easier to build.",
            [
                new("What is the text mainly about?", ["The life of a famous American truck driver", "Why dockworkers stole goods from ships", "How bananas are grown and sold", "How the shipping container transformed world trade"], 3, "The text describes shipping before containers, how containers were introduced and their effects on the economy."),
                new("What did McLean's ship carry in 1956?", ["Sacks and barrels of different sizes", "Fifty-eight metal boxes that could be lifted from lorries", "Oil for the city of Houston", "Parts for building container terminals"], 1, "Paragraph 3 says it carried \"fifty-eight metal boxes that could be lifted straight from lorries onto the ship\"."),
                new("In paragraph 3, what does \"standardisation\" refer to?", ["Making containers from stronger steel", "Paying all dockworkers the same wage", "Agreeing on common sizes and fittings for containers", "Building every port in the same city"], 2, "The text explains it as international agreements that \"fixed common sizes and fittings\"."),
                new("What can we infer about manufacturing before containers?", ["Goods were usually made closer to where they were sold", "Most goods were made in several countries", "Factories were built inside ports", "Transport costs had no effect on where goods were made"], 0, "Paragraph 2 says high transport costs meant it \"made no sense to manufacture them far from where they would be sold\"."),
            ])),
    ];
